course: dictionary
lesson: 14401
title: The outset
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The outset means the start or beginning of something. Writers use it in stories, news and exams, where it sounds a little more formal than start. «From the outset, it was clear the plan would fail.» From the very beginning, the plan was going to fail.
explain: Look for the clue words «at» and «from». «At the outset» means at the start. «From the outset» means from the start, right from the first moment. Both phrases point to the beginning.
explain: In everyday talk you can say «at the start» or «from the beginning» instead. If one of those fits the sentence and still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Outset means the start or beginning of something.
fact: «At the outset» means at the start.
fact: «From the outset» means from the start, right from the beginning.
fact: Outset is a noun, and it is only used in the singular: the outset, not outsets.
fact: Everyday words that mean the same as outset are start and beginning.
exercise: «The team struggled from the outset of the match.» What does outset mean here? | The half-time break | The end | *The start | The final score
exercise: Ella asks, «What does at the outset mean?» | Right at the end | After a long rest | In the middle of something | *Right at the start
exercise: «The race began, and at the outset the runners were close together.» Find and copy one word that means start. | runners | close | *outset | together
test: «From the outset, Sam knew the cake would be delicious.» What does from the outset mean? | *From the very start | After it was eaten | Halfway through | From the oven
test: Which word could replace outset in «the outset of the trip»? | ending | dinner | *beginning | middle
exam: Which word is the best swap for outset in «At the outset, we were hopeful»? | At the side | *At the start | At the bottom | At the back
